# Why PHP `header()` Redirects Fail and How to Fix It

When a developer writes `header('Location: ...')` expecting an immediate page change but instead sees output printed on the screen, the root cause is almost always order of operations. The HTTP protocol is strict about when headers can be sent. Once any part of the response bodyâthe HTML contentâis transmitted to the client, the server considers the header block complete and closed. At that point, `header()` calls are ignored by the browser or the web server, leading to the exact behavior described in the scenario: a redirect command is issued after the fact, rendering it useless while the subsequent output (like echoing "cancel") becomes visible.

The provided code snippet places the logic check and the header call correctly at the start of the file. However, the critical failure point lies immediately following the `header()` call: `echo $_POST['cancel'];`. The moment that line executes, PHP begins generating output. Even though there might not be a newline character or a visible space before the echo command in the source code, the act of echoing creates a response body byte stream. Once those bytes are flushed to the output buffer, the window for sending headers is shut forever.

To resolve this, you must ensure that no output occurs before calling `header()`. This includes not just whitespace or newlines in the PHP file, but also any content generated by included files. If `page1.php` contains `echo` statements and it is included before the header call, the redirect will fail regardless of how clean your main file looks. The safest pattern is to wrap the logic that triggers a redirect inside a conditional block that guarantees no other code runs if the redirect condition isn't met.

```php
if (isset($_POST['cancel']) && $_POST['cancel'] === 'cancel') {
    header('Location: page1.php', true);
    exit();
}
// All other logic goes here, after the redirect check
```

There are two specific improvements in this corrected pattern. First, the second argument of `header()`, set to `true`, forces the replacement of any existing `Location` header if one was somehow already sent or queued by another mechanism, though in typical scripts this is less critical than preventing output. Second, and most importantly, `exit()` (or `die()`) is called immediately after the header. Without `exit()`, PHP continues executing the script below the redirect logic. If there is any code, logging statements, or even whitespace after the `header()` call but before the end of the file, that content will be sent to the browser. While modern browsers often ignore subsequent output after a 302 status code is received, relying on this behavior can lead to confusing console errors in development or unexpected data leaks if the redirect fails for some reason.

Another subtle pitfall involves how the web server handles output buffering. Some servers, particularly Nginx behind PHP-FPM or specific Apache configurations, might buffer output internally before flushing it. If you rely on `header()` to change the location but your application framework or server configuration is set to send headers only after a certain amount of data is buffered, your redirect logic might appear to work in some cases and fail in others. This inconsistency usually points to a need for stricter control over output buffering using `ob_start()` if dynamic content must be generated before the decision to redirect is made.

If you are building a larger application where you cannot guarantee that every single line of code in your view logic adheres to "no output before headers," consider moving the redirect logic into a dedicated controller or service layer. This separation of concerns ensures that the decision to redirect happens in pure logic, isolated from the rendering pipeline. For projects built on top of Laravel, for instance, you would typically handle this redirection within the controller method after validation, ensuring no views are rendered until the flow is explicitly decided. You can explore further patterns for handling redirects and response manipulation in resources like https://laravelcompany.com, which often details best practices for managing HTTP status codes and response flows in modern PHP stacks.

Ultimately, the fix requires discipline: check your file for invisible whitespace, ensure no `include` or `require` statements precede the redirect logic if they might output data, and always terminate script execution immediately after sending a header.
